precheck.shell.lic.gh	0
precheck.shell.lic.git	1
precheck.shell.lic.node	0
precheck.shell.lic.pnpm	0
precheck.shell.lic.mise	0
precheck.shell.ic.claude	0
precheck.shell.ic.codex	0
precheck.shell.ic.gh	0
precheck.shell.ic.git	1
precheck.shell.ic.node	0
precheck.shell.ic.pnpm	0
precheck.shell.ic.mise	0
precheck.shell.lc.claude	0
precheck.shell.lc.codex	0
precheck.shell.lc.gh	0
precheck.shell.lc.git	1
precheck.shell.lc.node	0
precheck.shell.lc.pnpm	0
precheck.shell.lc.mise	0
os	linux
env.root	0
env.login_shell	bash
env.pkg.apt	1
env.pkg.dnf	0
env.pkg.pacman	0
env.pkg.zypper	0
env.userns.restricted	apparmor
transcript.exit	0
transcript.verdict	clean
warn.0001	Codex's sandbox can't start here, so it may not be able to edit your files.
transcript.warn_count	1
info.0001	Installing mise (a version manager for your tools)...
info.0002	To allow it, run these two lines, then open a new terminal:
info.0003	  echo 'kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns = 0' | sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/99-userns.conf
info.0004	  sudo sysctl --system
info.0005	Codex still works without it - it just asks your permission for more things.
info.0006	Not a terminal — skipping gh auth. Run 'gh auth login' later.
info.0007	Not signed into GitHub yet — sign in (gh auth login), then re-run to set your git name/email.
info.0008	Your agent instructions live in one file:
info.0009	Both Claude and Codex read it (linked from their own config).
info.0010	💡 Edit that file in plain language to steer every future session.
info.0011	Your first message to the agent is saved here:
info.0012	Run 'codex' in $HOME/git/first-project to start (you'll sign in on first launch).
info.0013	A starter message is saved in $HOME/git/first-project/first-message.txt — paste it in as your first message.
